# lemmas about the primitive identity type `=`, whose only proof is `backbend`.
# matching on `backbend` replaceth one side of an equation with the other.

## equality is preserved by applying a function.
show let leibnitz: [
  @α: *, @β: *,
  @a: α, @b: α,
  f: [α, β], backbend: a = b, (a f) = (b f)
] ≔ backbend

## reverse an equality proof.
show let evenmete: [
  @α: *, @a: α, @b: α,
  backbend: a = b, b = a
] ≔ backbend

## join two equality proofs.
show let throughgo: [
  @α: *, @a: α, @b: α, @c: α,
  backbend: a = b, q: b = c, a = c
] ≔ q

## a property of one side holdeth for the other. the property is found by
## abstracting each occurrence of `b` in the expected type, or without one,
## each occurrence of `a` in the type of the second input.
show let transport: [
  @α: *, @a: α, @b: α, @property: [α, *],
  backbend: a = b, a property, b property
] ≔ {x ^ x}

## a computed value with the proof of what it is, so that a case function can
## match the value and still know it: in
## `e inspect { yea, known ^ …, nay, known ^ … }` each case seeth `known`
## as `e = yea` or `e = nay`. the property is found as for `transport`.
show let inspect: [
  @α: *, @property: [α, *],
  a: α, f: [b: α, a = b, b property], a property
] ≔ a f backbend
